00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:51.420
Ok, now, let's take a look at 15 crazy fun
facts and easter eggs in the movie Trolls.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.789
Because hair is such an important and distinctive
part of how the Trolls look, the animators

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:58.449
wanted to make sure they got it just right
in the movie.

00:00:58.449 --> 00:01:03.269
So, they filmed slow-motion footage of the
original Troll toys and used it as a reference

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:04.940
to see how their hair moved.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:09.790
In fact, the movie had a dedicated Hair Task
Force, with hundreds of artists and technicians

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:11.680
working solely on hair effects.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.500
And the DreamWorks tech team even created
a brand new piece of software called Willow

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.340
to help make it easier for the movie's artists
to style, simulate and preview the Trolls'

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:22.340
hair.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:27.869
In total, the animators created 1.8 million
strands of hair for the movie.

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:32.759
To give you an idea how that works out for
individual characters, Poppy has almost 84,000

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:36.229
strands, while Branch has nearly 50,000 strands.

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:40.930
But the character who has the most hair on
her head is Lady Glittersparkles, whose overall

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:47.880
style is pure 70s disco diva, and whose rainbow
wig is made up of over 230,000 strands!

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.770
But even Lady Glittersparkles can't compete
with the number of strands required to create

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:58.420
the hair plus all the fur on the giraffe-like
Troll, Cooper, because his head and body took

00:01:58.420 --> 00:01:59.420
over 800,000 strands!

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:07.289
Not only did the movie's crew have their work
cut out creating masses of super-powered Troll

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:10.540
hair for the film, they also had to deal with
tons of glitter.

00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:15.239
And that was not easy as their computers could
only make glitter look realistic when it was

00:02:15.239 --> 00:02:19.480
motionless, and not when it was moving around
and reflecting light as it needed to do in

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:20.480
the movie.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.230
To solve this sparkly problem, DreamWorks
tech team put on their thinking hats again

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:28.480
and invented another new piece of software,
the GlitterFlakes Shader, which made glitter

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.909
flakes in the movie look randomly scattered
about, as they'd naturally look in real life,

00:02:32.909 --> 00:02:38.900
but also allowed the movie's artists to easily
manipulate their movement, shape and reflectiveness.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.510
If there's one thing that isn't sparkly in
the Trolls movie, it's the unhappy, ogre-like

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:44.510
Bergens.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.030
When the animators were designing the gloomy
land where the Bergens live, they turned to

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.470
1970s suburbia for inspiration, which is why
the Bergens' world is mainly coloured in earth

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:58.069
tones like shades of burnt orange, dark green,
and gold, and that's also why Bergentown is

00:02:58.069 --> 00:03:03.409
influenced by popular 1970s things like shag
carpet, wood panelling, polyester, Formica,

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:04.880
and Brutalist architecture.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.439
But the 1970s wasn't the only era that influenced
the look of Bergentown.

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:14.000
The animators also looked further back, to
the Middle Ages, and used the layout of walled

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.019
medieval villages as a reference for how they
arranged Bergentown.

00:03:18.019 --> 00:03:23.610
Although the technology exists to make animated
movies look more photo-realistic than ever

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:28.079
before – think of the landscapes in Pixar's
The Good Dinosaur, for example – the filmmakers

00:03:28.079 --> 00:03:34.030
behind Trolls wanted their movie to have a
very handmade, textured feel, so, to achieve

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.930
that, they based the film's look on felt,
fabrics, and fibre art.

00:03:37.930 --> 00:03:41.930
Which means that when the animators were creating
the Trolls' super-fuzzy world, things like

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:46.879
macramé, felting techniques, flocked material,
velvet, and knitting were a big influence

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:47.989
on its look.

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:51.910
And to give the crew a proper feel for how
the Trolls world should look, the film's production

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:57.140
designer asked Iowa-based artist Sayuri Sasaki
Hemann to create a model of the Troll forest

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.390
out of fibre and fabrics.

00:04:00.390 --> 00:04:04.910
The hit song Can't Stop The Feeling was co-written
by Justin Timberlake, who voices Branch, to

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:09.030
resolve a problem the filmmakers were having
with how to change the Bergens' glum outlook

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.489
and bring the film to a satisfying close.

00:04:11.489 --> 00:04:15.180
The directors didn't like the idea of the
Bergens being transformed after hearing a

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:19.430
character give a motivational speech and they
couldn't find a song to fit the moment, so

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.440
they asked Timberlake to write a song that
would have the right effect and Can't Stop

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:24.870
the Feeling was the result!

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:29.280
As director Mike Mitchell says, the song 'creates
happiness when you hear it', so it makes sense

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.190
that it would bring the Trolls and Bergens
together in the movie.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.660
And in keeping with the movie's 1970s feel,
Timberlake, who's also the film's Executive

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.970
Music Producer, channelled a mix of Michael
Jackson, the Bee Gees, and Bill Withers when

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.580
he wrote the song.

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:48.199
When it came to getting the right vibe between
Poppy and Branch, who start off as polar opposites

00:04:48.199 --> 00:04:52.820
and are both very determined characters, the
filmmakers took cues from the classic 80s

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:57.430
action-comedy Romancing the Stone, specifically
the on-screen energy and back-and-forth between

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.660
its stars Kathleen Turner and Michael Douglas.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.800
In fact, Romancing the Stone is actually one
of Trolls director Mike Mitchell's favourite

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.639
movies.

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:10.270
According to the directors of Trolls, their
movie's "strange fairytale-like dream" quality

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:14.870
owes a great deal to the odd creatures and
dream-like worlds created by renowned Japanese

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:20.110
writer-director Hayao Miyazaki, who co-founded
Studio Ghibli and gave us such classic animated

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:21.479
movies as Spirited Away.

00:05:21.479 --> 00:05:26.120
And for the scene where Poppy sings "The Sound
of Silence" accompanied by a variety of weird

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.259
and wonderful creatures and plant life, the
filmmakers channelled not only Miyazaki, but

00:05:30.259 --> 00:05:33.300
also the animated TV show Adventure Time.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.080
And director Mike Mitchell has said that in
this scene, Poppy is like a "quirky twist

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.509
on Snow White or Cinderella, who are friendly
with all the forest creatures".

00:05:41.509 --> 00:05:45.901
At the start of the movie, when Chef plucks
what she believes to be a Troll from the Troll

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:50.229
tree for the young Prince Gristle to eat,
the Bergens are all horrified to discover

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:55.169
that it's not a real-life Troll, but a wooden
one, as the real Trolls have cunningly escaped.

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:59.160
This moment is a lovely Easter Egg and tribute
to Thomas Dam, the Danish creator of the original

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.440
Troll toys that the movie is based on as the
first funny little figures that he made were

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:04.440
carved out of wood.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.910
And it was the popularity of those early wood
carvings that led Dam to create the very first

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:09.580
Troll toys we know today!

00:06:09.580 --> 00:06:14.720
And as a little Easter Egg and homage to the
Troll dolls that became so popular in the

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.849
1960s and often didn't wear any clothes, the
filmmakers invented a character for the movie

00:06:18.849 --> 00:06:22.330
called Guy Diamond, who's naked except for
glitter.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:27.820
By the way, the character of Guy Diamond is
voiced by The Big Bang Theory's Kunal Nayyar.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:31.229
Trolls also makes references to a number of
other movies and stories.

00:06:31.229 --> 00:06:35.270
For example, when Poppy leaves the Troll village
and heads to Bergentown to save her friends,

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:38.880
she crosses paths with a bunch of spiders,
and falls through a series of webs getting

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.979
completely wrapped up in one of them.

00:06:40.979 --> 00:06:45.330
That scene in the movie really tips its hat
to Peter Jackson's movie The Hobbit: The Desolation

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:49.410
of Smaug, and in turn, of course, to JRR Tolkien's
original novel The Hobbit.

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:53.980
And there's the scene where scullery maid
Bridget, who's disguised as Lady Glittersparkles,

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.740
is on a date with King Gristle Jr and she
gets all tongue-tied, so the Trolls help her

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.389
out telling her what to say.

00:07:00.389 --> 00:07:04.389
Well, that scene is a nod to the French play
Cyrano de Bergerac, which has been adapted

00:07:04.389 --> 00:07:09.570
in many movies over the years, including the
1987 hit comedy Roxanne starring Steve Martin

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:10.780
and Daryl Hannah.

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:16.330
And when Lady Glittersparkles accidentally
leaves behind one of her roller skates after

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:20.430
her date with King Gristle Jr, it's a clear
shout-out to the story of Cinderella, which

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:25.110
has also been adapted for the big screen many
times, including Disney's live-action version

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:29.800
with Lily James and Richard Madden, and Anna
Kendrick also played Cinderella in Disney's

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.370
version of Into The Woods.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:36.259
Speaking of roller skating, for the design
of Captain Starfunkle's, the filmmakers took

00:07:36.259 --> 00:07:40.509
cues from Moonlight Rollerway, a roller skating
rink which is just a stone's throw from the

00:07:40.509 --> 00:07:43.080
DreamWorks Animation offices in Glendale,
California.

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.840
And, actually, the movie's crew went skating
there several times while they were making

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:47.840
the film!

00:07:47.840 --> 00:07:52.680
As well as co-directing the movie, Walt Dohrn
also lends his voice to a number of small

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:57.539
characters including Smidge, who's a little
Troll with a very deep voice; Mr Dinkles,

00:07:57.539 --> 00:08:02.050
who's Biggie's pet worm; Fuzzbert, a Troll
who's mostly hair with a pair of feet sticking

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:06.830
out; and Cloud Guy, the white sock-wearing,
walking, talking cloud who loves high-fiving

00:08:06.830 --> 00:08:07.830
everyone!

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:12.220
Dohrn's co-director, Mike Mitchell also stepped
into the sound booth to record various voices

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:16.740
for the film including a Spider, and Captain
Starfunkle, who serves pizza to King Gristle

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:18.860
Jr and Lady Glittersparkles during their date.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:19.860
And the directors kept some of the other movie's
cameos in the family with Iris Dohrn as Baby

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:20.860
Poppy, Phoebe Dohrn as a Bergen Girl, and
Ollie and Emmett Mitchell as trolls.

